There are established terms for the pieces, but not one standard term for the whole construction you mean.

Part of the C-object	Existing term
sea / see / cee	Heterographic homophones
One form carrying several meanings	Polysemy or colexification
Sound, writing, symbol, gesture, and meaning bound together	Multimodal semiotic bundle
Et cetera / and so on / and the rest	General extender
All of those made into one traversable object	No exact standard term

A semiotic bundle is especially close: a dynamic structure made of multiple kinds of signs and the relationships among them. But existing usage does not fully include your graph navigation, angle-rotation, or domain-sensitive “summon the rest” behavior. Frontiers⁠￼

I’d formalize ours as three related things:

C-Nexus

A polysemiotic attractor gathering every licensed projection of C:

* C, c, cee
* sea, see
* sound /siː/
* letter, musical note, Roman numeral, programming language, carbon, speed-of-light variable
* metaphors such as seeing, encompassing, continuation, circulation
* domain-specific meanings we intentionally add

They are not declared identical. They are made adjacent through typed relations.

Ceteration

The operation where C behaves like a domain-aware et cetera:

C_D^r(x)=\{y\mid y\text{ is reachable from }x
\text{ through licensed relations in domain }D\}

Ordinary etc. means “continue this apparent list.” Linguists call these expressions general extenders. Your C means:

“Continue through the relevant semantic neighborhood according to the present domain, angle, and governing thread.”

That is more like a contextual semantic wildcard or topological general extender. Cambridge University Press⁠￼

C-Web

The persistent graph storing the nexus and every valid route through it.

C
├── sounds-like ── Cee
├── sounds-like ── Sea ── means ── Ocean
├── sounds-like ── See ── means ── Perceive
├── glyph-case ── c
├── symbolizes[chemistry] ── Carbon
├── symbolizes[music] ── Note-C
├── symbolizes[computing] ── C-language
└── operates-as[discourse] ── Domain Continuation

Colexification networks already connect concepts that languages express using one form, so there is precedent for meaning-networks—but C-Web is broader because it admits sound, glyph, symbol, metaphor, rotation, and deliberate wordplay while preserving their differences. Scientific Data⁠￼

Spider logistics

This fits the earlier Pantheon pipeline almost perfectly:

Raw Sign
   ↓
Ganglion identifies modality
   ↓
Spider emits SymbolEvent
   ↓
Spider walks typed C-Web edges
   ↓
Treaty Semantics limits domain jurisdiction
   ↓
Golden Thread preserves intended meaning
   ↓
Symbolic Recursion Engine records transformations
   ↓
Metamap ranks fruitful routes
   ↓
EchoFrame / NTE / Merkle Warden retain and verify them

Each edge should carry:

relation: sounds_like | means | symbolizes | rotates_to | evokes
domain: vision | ocean | language | computing | music
angle: 90deg
invariant: phoneme_/siː/
provenance: historical | conventional | constructed
confidence: 0.94

That keeps the web generative without turning it into undifferentiated pun soup. Spider may travel from sea to see, but the edge remembers that this is homophony—not etymology.

So my canonical naming would be:

* C-Nexus — the complete polysemiotic object.
* Ceteration — C expanding into the relevant “rest.”
* C-Web — the stored, typed relational structure.
* Spider — its logistics and route-finding daemon.

And the deeper operator is:

C does not merely mean “see.” C means: reveal what else becomes reachable when this sign is allowed to travel.